ome engraving and parts resolve into confident contemporary illustration without ever looking like separate styles pasted together','Create a major contemporary illustrative tattoo in which a human figure stands within an impossible landscape of enormous vegetation distant architecture and a massive animal moving through the environment. Use changing drawing language to control depth: precise linework in the focal figure expressive contour in the animal engraved hatching in selected landscape areas unfinished construction marks at the outer boundaries and intentional untouched skin throughout. Every mark should feel authored. The composition should resemble an extraordinary original illustration created specifically for the human body rather than a photograph collage or conventional tattoo flash')
$b['A horses head is captured with energetic sweeping contours rather than enclosed outlines. Several exploratory lines remain visible around the neck and jaw while darker decisive marks establish the final form'] = 'The tattoo: A horses head is captured with energetic sweeping contours rather than enclosed outlines. Several exploratory lines remain visible around the neck and jaw while darker decisive marks establish the final form. Expressive, elegant, alive. Create exceptional contemporary Illustrative tattoo artwork in which the visible act of drawing is fundamental to the finished tattoo. The image must clearly read as authored illustration rather than photographic realism, conventional tattoo flash or generic digital artwork. Explore genuinely different drawing voices across the collection including confident contour drawing, expressive pen work, pencil-like construction, sketchbook drawing, cross-hatching, parallel hatching, stippling, engraving, etching, woodcut-influenced mark making, charcoal-like gesture, editorial illustration, botanical illustration, figurative drawing, graphic-novel-influenced composition and contemporary fine-art drawing. Do not force every image into engraving. Do not force every image into Fine Line. Allow line weight, mark density, texture, black structure and degree of finish to change substantially from design to design. Keep evidence of the artists hand visible through searching lines, doubled contours, construction marks, broken edges, hatching direction, deliberate simplification, selective unfinished areas and expressive decisions. Subjects may be completely conventional or highly imaginative: people, human figures, portraits, hands, animals, birds, botanicals, landscapes, architecture, mythology, machines, ocean, intimate scenes, narrative moments, invented creatures and surreal transformations are all valid. Creativity should come from drawing, composition, viewpoint, scale relationships, transformation, emotional storytelling and the artists interpretation rather than comedy or random novelty objects. Some designs may be black only, some black and grey, some restrained color and some richly colored. When color is used it should participate in the illustration rather than conceal the drawing. Match complexity to physical size. Most pieces should be attainable small and medium tattoos suitable for forearms, upper arms, shoulders, calves and thighs. Small pieces should use strong readable drawing rather than compressed excessive detail. Medium pieces should form the heart of the collection. Use fewer large works and only rare major compositions. Design intelligently for the stated body placement. Avoid photorealistic rendering, generic AI concept art, clip art, repetitive engravings, repetitive skulls, repetitive moths, generic Pinterest flash, random geometric overlays, meaningless scribbles, comedy as the default creative device and the assumption that more detail automatically means better illustration. The goal is for every piece to make the viewer feel that a remarkable illustrator personally drew something worth permanently wearing; no watermark. In the right panel it is on a woman, inner forearm'
